findings.evidence:
deepSet walks the key path and asserts each intermediate node is a map without the comma-ok form:

  939 func deepSet(m map[string]any, keys []string, value any) {
  940   for i := 0; i < len(keys)-1; i++ {
  941     key := keys[i]
  942     if _, ok := m[key]; !ok {
  943       m[key] = make(map[string]any)
  944     }
  945     m = m[key].(map[string]any)

Reached end to end from the wire. For a parameter declared `in: query, style: deepObject` with an object schema - a combination openapi3/parameter.go accepts as valid (`ParameterInQuery && SerializationDeepObject && sm.Explode`, with Explode defaulting to true for query parameters at openapi3/parameter.go SerializationMethod) - ValidateRequest -> ValidateParameter -> decodeStyledParameter -> urlValuesDecoder.DecodeObject (req_resp_decoder.go:697-727) builds props by joining every `[...]` capture with \x1F, then makeObject (:951-962) iterates props and calls deepSet for each. Query string `?p[a]=1&p[a][b]=2` yields props {"a": "1", "a\x1Fb": "2"}. Go map iteration order is randomized: when "a" is visited first, mobj["a"] holds the string "1", and the next key's loop executes `m["a"].(map[string]any)` on a string, which panics by construction. The guard at :956 inspects the value for the delimiter, never the keys, and grep for `recover()` across all 293 Go files returns nothing.
The race can be removed by the attacker: with `?p[a]=1&p[a][k1]=v&...&p[a][k50]=v` the panic occurs unless "a" is the last of 51 keys visited, i.e. on about 98% of requests.
No test exercises this shape: TestDeepSet (req_resp_decoder_test.go:228-278) covers only fresh and already-map intermediates, and no deepObject case in req_resp_decoder_test.go supplies a scalar and a deeper key under the same prefix.

The loader keys its in-flight $ref set and its deferred backtrack callbacks on the ref STRING alone, with no record of the component type that registered them:

  427 func (loader *Loader) shouldVisitRef(ref string, fn func(value any)) bool {
  428   if _, ok := loader.visitedRefs[ref]; ok {
  429     loader.backtrack[ref] = append(loader.backtrack[ref], fn)
  430     return false
  ...
  416 func (loader *Loader) unvisitRef(ref string, value any) {
  417   if value != nil {
  418     for _, fn := range loader.backtrack[ref] { fn(value) }

Each of the nine resolve*Ref functions registers a callback that asserts its own type without the comma-ok form: loader.go:744 `value.(*Header)`, :815 `value.(*Parameter)`, :894 `*RequestBody`, :960 `*Response`, :1052 `*Schema`, :1249 `*SecurityScheme`, :1306 `*Example`, :1352 `*Callback`, :1418 `*Link`, :1465 `*PathItem`. Registration happens before any type check, because shouldVisitRef returns false without drilling into the document.
The strict type check at loader.go:540 normally prevents two component types from resolving the same ref, but loader.go:546-561 exempts anything that drills out as map[string]any and JSON round-trips it into whatever type the caller asked for. Any unknown top-level key lands in T.Extensions (openapi3.go:17-19 declares Extensions as field 0; UnmarshalJSON copies every unrecognised key into it), and drillIntoField reaches it at loader.go:668-674. A document can therefore point two different component kinds at one extension node:

  openapi: "3.0.0"
  info: {title: t, version: "1"}
  paths:
    /a: {get: {responses: {"200": {$ref: "#/x-thing"}}}}
  x-thing:
    description: d
    headers: {H: {$ref: "#/x-thing"}}

Trace: resolveResponseRef(:955) finds the ref unvisited, calls visitRef, resolveComponent drills T.Extensions["x-thing"] to a map[string]any and round-trips it into a ResponseRef whose Value carries Headers{H: &HeaderRef{Ref:"#/x-thing"}}. The inner resolveResponseRef then iterates value.Headers (:998-1003) and calls resolveHeaderRef, whose shouldVisitRef sees the ref already in flight and appends the `value.(*Header)` closure from :744 to backtrack["#/x-thing"]. Control returns; component.Value is set to the *Response at :987; the deferred `unvisitRef(ref, component.Value)` at :990 then invokes that closure with a *Response, producing `interface conversion: interface {} is *openapi3.Response, not *openapi3.Header`. The symmetric ParameterRef/SchemaRef pairing panics the same way at :1052.

Rendering a validation error re-encodes the rejected value and panics if the encoder refuses it:

  3192 if !SchemaErrorDetailsDisabled {
  3193   buf.WriteString("\nSchema:\n  ")
  3194   encoder := json.NewEncoder(buf)
  3196   if err := encoder.Encode(err.Schema); err != nil { panic(err) }
  3199   buf.WriteString("\nValue:\n  ")
  3200   if err := encoder.Encode(err.Value); err != nil { panic(err) }

SchemaErrorDetailsDisabled is false by default (schema.go:34), so the branch is live out of the box.
The value reaching err.Value is not restricted to JSON-decoded types. openapi3filter registers YamlBodyDecoder for application/yaml and application/x-yaml (req_resp_decoder.go:1412-1413), and visitJSON carries a dedicated branch for the YAML decoder's non-JSON map type:

  2023 case map[any]any: // for YAML cf. issue .../issues/444
  2024   values := make(map[string]any, len(value))
  2025   for key, v := range value { if k, ok := key.(string); ok { values[k] = v } }
  2030   if len(value) == len(values) { return schema.visitJSONObject(settings, values) }

When any key is not a string the branch does not return; control falls through the reflect.Slice check to schema.go:2045-2051, which builds `&SchemaError{Value: value, ...}` carrying the map[any]any. encoding/json cannot marshal a map whose key type is interface{}, so `encoder.Encode(err.Value)` returns *json.UnsupportedTypeError and line 3201 panics.
The error is rendered on both entry points: openapi3filter/middleware.go:135 calls the default logFunc, which is `log.Printf("%s: %v", message, err)` (middleware.go:68-70), and openapi3filter/validation_handler.go:77 calls DefaultErrorEncoder, which does `[]byte(err.Error())` (validation_kit.go:65). RequestError.Error() forwards to the wrapped SchemaError at openapi3filter/errors.go:25-31.
A request body such as `1: a` sent with `Content-Type: application/yaml` against an operation whose requestBody declares that media type with a non-empty schema is the whole payload. A second trigger of the same root cause: a YAML body containing `.nan` inside a value that a SchemaError reports whole - encoding/json also refuses NaN.

The only built-in read policy is a single boolean, and once it is on the reader accepts anything the document names:

   46 var DefaultReadFromURI = URIMapCache(ReadFromURIs(ReadFromHTTP(http.DefaultClient), ReadFromFile))
   51 func ReadFromHTTP(cl *http.Client) ReadFromURIFunc {
   52   return func(loader *Loader, location *url.URL) ([]byte, error) {
   53     if location.Scheme == "" || location.Host == "" { return nil, ErrURINotSupported }
   56     req, err := http.NewRequest("GET", location.String(), nil)
   60     resp, err := cl.Do(req)
   ...
   79 func ReadFromFile(loader *Loader, location *url.URL) ([]byte, error) {
   80   if !is_file(location) { return nil, ErrURINotSupported }
   83   return os.ReadFile(path.Clean(filepath.FromSlash(location.Path)))

There is no allowlist of hosts or schemes, no rejection of loopback, link-local or RFC1918 destinations, no base-directory containment for the file reader, and no redirect policy: http.DefaultClient follows up to ten redirects, so a permitted host can redirect the loader to 169.254.169.254 or to any internal address. ReadFromFile applies path.Clean but never compares the result against a root, so `$ref: "/etc/passwd"` and `$ref: "../../secrets.yml"` resolve to whatever the process can read; loader.go:348-360 sends an absolute componentPath straight through resolvePath without joining it to the base.
http.DefaultClient carries no Timeout, so a single $ref pointed at a host that accepts the connection and never responds blocks the load indefinitely; loader.Context is stored (loader.go:58) but never attached to the request at loader_uri_reader.go:56, so cancelling it does not stop the fetch.
The project is aware of the class for the custom-reader case and says so in the ReadFromURIFunc field comment (loader.go:41-48: 'enables local file reads (`$ref: "/etc/passwd"`) and SSRF (`$ref: "http://169.254.169.254/..."`)'). The same property holds for the built-in default once IsExternalRefsAllowed is set, and that is what the README's own quickstart sets: README.md:132 `loader := &openapi3.Loader{Context: ctx, IsExternalRefsAllowed: true}`. A consumer following the documented example and loading a document from an untrusted source gets the full behaviour with no further opt-in.
